  "notes": "\"Beach country\" — island-tinged midtempo party records alternating with sentimental ballads. The key weighting is a documented fact rather than a guess: \"G major dominating his catalogue more than any other key here, then E/D/F/F♯.\" The perc lane set is the entry's own instrument list — \"the beach lane uses a light, dry kit with congas/bongos, shaker and steel-drum-adjacent percussion over a two-beat; the rock lane uses quarter kick and 8th hats\" — so conga and bongo are authored as lanes, which nothing else in this section carries. The room philosophy is documented and reaches the humanize block: rooms are chosen for \"full-band playback comfort rather than acoustic optimisation,\" i.e. a live-in-one-room ensemble feel with modest isolation. Harmony is the stated \"I–V–vi–IV and I–IV–V, with a IV–I plagal loop under the island cuts\" — that two-chord plagal loop is the family that makes the beach lane audible. The bass has a real structural habit: \"the bass frequently sits out the first verse entirely,\" and the melody is \"chantable by a stadium\" — a fourth or fifth up on a repeated rhythmic cell. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium); the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF] and the entry notes songdata's 144–176 figures fold into the stated band. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the perc density and gain, the humanize values and the counter ratio.",
